  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 409 PM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nMid-Week...\n\nFor the Tuesday through Thursday time frame, overall no notable \nchanges in the forecast. Models show little change in the upper \nlevel pattern, with the main upper level ridge axis continuing \nto run roughly through the High Plains. There remain periodic \nchances for showers and thunderstorms...mainly during the mid-\nlate evening and overnight hours...as models continue to show \nthe potential for shortwave disturbances riding around the edge \nof the main ridging and sliding through the area. Precipitation \nchances Tue evening-night and again Wed evening-night remain \ngenerally in the 20-50 percent range...with models then showing \nthe potential for a stronger shortwave disturbance to dig \nfurther south, with more widespread 40-60 percent chances Thu- \nThu night. Still plenty of details to iron out with the \ntiming/coverage of those chances. \n\nMore noteworthy for the mid-week period is models showing a\nbreak from the heat from the past few days. Highs Tue-Thu are \nforecast in the upper 80s-mid 90s...with heat index values more \nin the 90s-near 100...vs the well above 100 from the past few \ndays.\n\nFriday and on...\n\nThe forecast for Friday on into the weekend dries out...but will\nsee if that ultimately ends up panning out that way. The upper\nlevel disturbance that moves through Thu-Thu night looks to \ngive the main ridge axis a better push back to the \nwest...leaving our area with more amplified north-northwesterly \nflow. At this point models aren't showing much in the way of \ndisturbances impacting the area. This shift in the ridging helps\nkeeps temps closer to normal for this time of year...generally \nforecast to range from the mid 80s to near 90 through this \nperiod.",
